1
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:06,910
Okay. Get comfortable. Because you will not believe what Cella did this week.

2
00:00:07,090 --> 00:00:08,750
Is this the fasting thing?

3
00:00:08,930 --> 00:00:10,350
It's the fasting thing.

4
00:00:10,370 --> 00:00:16,520
I knew it. She bought a cute water bottle on Sunday. That's how it starts.

5
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:22,470
That is exactly how it starts. A bottle. Then a calendar. Then a personality.

6
00:00:22,650 --> 00:00:26,850
Tell me everything. Slowly. I have tea.

7
00:00:27,030 --> 00:00:28,840
You always have tea.

8
00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:34,110
Tea is my fasting. I fast from drama by listening to drama.

9
00:00:34,290 --> 00:00:35,260
That's not a thing.

10
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:36,680
It's my thing.

11
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:48,030
So Monday morning, the whole house is in the kitchen, and Cella walks in. No breakfast. Just stands there. Like a press conference.

12
00:00:48,210 --> 00:00:49,710
Did she clear her throat?

13
00:00:49,890 --> 00:00:54,370
She cleared her throat. She said, "This week I'm doing the moon."

14
00:00:54,550 --> 00:00:55,790
Doing the moon.

15
00:00:55,810 --> 00:01:07,460
Doing the moon. Ekadashi. The eleventh day of the moon. It's a Hindu fast, people have done it for a very long time, and this Friday is one of them. Utpanna Ekadashi.

16
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:09,100
And she found this where?

17
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:16,810
She found a calendar. She has a moon calendar now. It's on the fridge. Facing the fridge. Like a warning.

18
00:01:16,990 --> 00:01:20,550
So what's the plan? She just doesn't eat until Friday?

19
00:01:20,730 --> 00:01:39,190
No, no. She's doing it like a week. The days before, she eats in a window. Ten hours, last bite by six p.m. And she was very clear that the ten hours is her own guess, not from the studies. The studies did eight to ten hours for twelve weeks, and one of them found no extra benefit from the timing at all.

20
00:01:39,370 --> 00:01:41,490
A window.

21
00:01:41,510 --> 00:01:45,640
A window. She says "eating window" like it's a balcony.

22
00:01:45,820 --> 00:01:47,220
And then Friday?

23
00:01:47,240 --> 00:01:56,480
Friday is the big one. Then Saturday she breaks it properly. There's a time for that too. She was very clear. She had a laminated card.

24
00:01:56,660 --> 00:01:58,090
Laminated.

25
00:01:58,110 --> 00:02:00,320
She owns a laminator now.

26
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:05,700
Okay, Tuesday. What happened Tuesday?

27
00:02:05,880 --> 00:02:09,700
Tuesday she ate dinner at five forty-five. Last bite by six.

28
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:12,430
That's an hour I usually call lunch.

29
00:02:12,610 --> 00:02:17,010
That's what I said! I said, Cella, that's lunch with the lights on.

30
00:02:17,190 --> 00:02:18,540
And did she survive?

31
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:25,130
She went to bed hungry. And smug. Hungry and smug is a very specific face.

32
00:02:25,310 --> 00:02:29,710
I know that face. That's the face of someone who did one push-up.

33
00:02:29,890 --> 00:02:33,150
She looked at the ceiling like the ceiling owed her an apology.

34
00:02:33,330 --> 00:02:33,980
Did it?

35
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,110
The ceiling said nothing. Respectfully.

36
00:02:39,890 --> 00:02:44,760
Now. Wednesday. This is the part I need you to really hear.

37
00:02:44,940 --> 00:02:45,740
I'm hearing.

38
00:02:45,760 --> 00:02:49,830
Hour twelve of the window being closed. She's standing in the kitchen.

39
00:02:50,010 --> 00:02:51,570
Eating?

40
00:02:51,590 --> 00:02:54,640
Not eating. Visiting the fridge.

41
00:02:54,820 --> 00:02:56,320
Visiting.

42
00:02:56,340 --> 00:03:01,520
She opens the door. She says hi to the cheese. She closes the door.

43
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:02,780
She says hi?

44
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:05,650
Out loud. "Hi." To the cheese.

45
00:03:05,830 --> 00:03:06,990
And the cheese?

46
00:03:07,010 --> 00:03:11,260
The cheese is very supportive. The cheese has been through a lot.

47
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:13,170
That's a hostage situation.

48
00:03:13,350 --> 00:03:17,770
And then. Hour fifteen. She opens the fridge one more time.

49
00:03:17,950 --> 00:03:18,760
No.

50
00:03:18,780 --> 00:03:20,270
And she closes it.

51
00:03:20,290 --> 00:03:21,450
Stop.

52
00:03:21,470 --> 00:03:28,120
She closed it. She went to bed hungry and smug. Again. Two nights in a row.

53
00:03:28,300 --> 00:03:30,330
Ugh. Now I have to respect her.

54
00:03:30,510 --> 00:03:33,760
That's the worst part of this week. The respect.

55
00:03:36,540 --> 00:03:38,240
Thursday's the day before, right?

56
00:03:38,420 --> 00:03:47,450
The eve. And on the eve, the tradition has its own rules. One simple meal before dusk. No lentils, no chickpeas, no beans.

57
00:03:47,630 --> 00:03:48,910
So she's prepping.

58
00:03:48,930 --> 00:03:55,840
She's prepping. Water by the bed. Curtains open, so the morning light hits her face. She read that the light helps.

59
00:03:56,020 --> 00:03:57,780
The light helps with what?

60
00:03:57,960 --> 00:04:05,780
With waking up. With being a person. It's part of the month she's doing. Light in the morning, water, the cold bucket thing.

61
00:04:05,960 --> 00:04:10,470
Oh, the bucket. We'll talk about the bucket another day.

62
00:04:10,650 --> 00:04:12,960
The bucket is a whole episode.

63
00:04:13,140 --> 00:04:16,700
Okay, but Thursday night. Be honest. Did we bet?

64
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:17,630
We bet.

65
00:04:17,650 --> 00:04:18,920
How much?

66
00:04:18,940 --> 00:04:24,680
Two coins. I said she caves before noon. You said she caves before dinner.

67
00:04:24,860 --> 00:04:28,660
I stand by dinner. Dinner is when the soul gets weak.

68
00:04:31,440 --> 00:04:34,520
Friday. Ekadashi. The day.

69
00:04:34,700 --> 00:04:36,180
The day of the moon.

70
00:04:36,200 --> 00:04:42,460
She wakes up. Light on her face. She drinks water. She looks at the calendar like it's a boss.

71
00:04:42,640 --> 00:04:46,600
And the fast is what, exactly? Nothing at all?

72
00:04:46,780 --> 00:05:01,850
Depends. People do it different ways. Water only. Fruit only. Milk. Or one meal without grains. Some people skip even water. And it's not for everybody, she read that part twice. Cella picked fruit and milk.

73
00:05:02,030 --> 00:05:04,700
Very reasonable for a woman with a laminator.

74
00:05:04,880 --> 00:05:10,560
So the morning goes fine. Ten a.m., fine. Noon, fine.

75
00:05:10,740 --> 00:05:12,060
There goes my coin.

76
00:05:12,080 --> 00:05:19,230
There goes your coin. Two p.m., she's reading. Four p.m., she's cleaning things that are already clean.

77
00:05:19,410 --> 00:05:21,400
Ah. The cleaning stage.

78
00:05:21,420 --> 00:05:27,090
The cleaning stage. Then seven fifteen p.m. She walks to the fridge.

79
00:05:27,270 --> 00:05:27,950
No.

80
00:05:27,970 --> 00:05:28,800
Opens it.

81
00:05:28,820 --> 00:05:29,390
No.

82
00:05:29,410 --> 00:05:32,720
And a piece of cheese just. Sticks to her.

83
00:05:32,900 --> 00:05:34,090
Sticks to her?

84
00:05:34,110 --> 00:05:39,480
She has no hands, it's a whole thing. The cheese commits. And she eats it.

85
00:05:39,660 --> 00:05:41,130
Caved.

86
00:05:41,150 --> 00:05:45,910
Caved at seven sixteen. I have it in writing. I wrote it on the moon calendar.

87
00:05:46,090 --> 00:05:47,100
So who won?

88
00:05:47,120 --> 00:05:52,330
Nobody won. That's the thing. Nobody won and everybody learned something.

89
00:05:52,510 --> 00:05:54,200
I hate learning.

90
00:05:56,820 --> 00:05:59,570
But here's what got me. Saturday.

91
00:05:59,750 --> 00:06:00,760
The next day.

92
00:06:00,780 --> 00:06:06,900
Most people, they cave, they say "okay, I'm a failure," they eat a whole pizza out of spite.

93
00:06:07,080 --> 00:06:09,400
Spite pizza. Classic.

94
00:06:09,420 --> 00:06:23,700
Not Cella. Saturday morning she gets up and does the breaking of the fast properly. There's a window for it, it's called pārana. In New Delhi it's six fifty-nine to nine oh four in the morning. Different where you live, so she looked up hers.

95
00:06:23,880 --> 00:06:27,100
So she failed and then did the ending right anyway.

96
00:06:27,280 --> 00:06:30,590
On time. With a spoon. Like a lady.

97
00:06:30,770 --> 00:06:34,430
That's honestly the most adult thing anyone in this house has done.

98
00:06:34,610 --> 00:06:36,330
I was furious.

99
00:06:38,950 --> 00:06:41,130
So Sunday. What's the verdict?

100
00:06:41,310 --> 00:06:45,590
Sunday she sits at the table with her laminated card and writes what worked.

101
00:06:45,770 --> 00:06:46,430
And?

102
00:06:46,450 --> 00:06:52,260
The early dinner worked. The morning light worked. The fridge visits did not work.

103
00:06:52,440 --> 00:06:55,860
The fridge visits were never going to work.

104
00:06:56,040 --> 00:07:07,010
And she wrote the next date. The next eleventh day. Mokshada Ekadashi. Saturday, December nineteenth if you're in the US. Sunday the twentieth in Delhi and London.

105
00:07:07,190 --> 00:07:08,440
She's doing it again?

106
00:07:08,460 --> 00:07:14,410
She's doing it again. She said, "I didn't fail the fast. I found out where the fridge is."

107
00:07:14,590 --> 00:07:16,730
She always knew where the fridge was.

108
00:07:16,910 --> 00:07:19,750
Now she knows where she is when she's at the fridge.

109
00:07:19,930 --> 00:07:23,300
Oh, that's annoying. That's annoyingly wise.

110
00:07:23,480 --> 00:07:24,640
I know.

111
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:26,710
Okay. Next Ekadashi?

112
00:07:26,730 --> 00:07:29,270
Next Ekadashi. Get comfortable.

